urrently, you could potentially draw a J1 later to make a pair, or a T2.\n\nBut after discarding... well, you don\u2019t discard until after Peng \u2014 when you Peng, you add the meld, then discard one tile.\n\nAh! Important rule: when you perform a **Peng**, you add the three tiles into a meld (exposed), then **discard one tile from your hand**.\n\nSo sequence:\n1. Opponent discards T5\n2. You choose Peng T5\n3. You reveal two T5s from hand \u2192 form [T5,T5,T5]\n4. Now your hand has: T2, J1 (and the other T5 is gone)\n5. Then you **must discard one tile**: either T2 or J1\n\nSo after Peng and discard, you\u2019ll have three tiles left: the other one plus...\n\nWait \u2014 no:\n- Before Peng: hand = T2, T5, T5, J1 (4 tiles)\n- You use two T5s for Peng \u2192 now hand = T2, J1\n- Then you **discard one**, say T2 \u2192 final hand = J1\n- And you have four melds \u2192 total tiles: 4\u00d73 + 1 = 13 \u2192 still not complete.\n\nBut to win, you need 14 tiles: 4 melds (12) + 1 pair (2) = 14.\n\nSo unless you **win immediately after the discard**, you can't win here.\n\nBut \u201cWin\u201d is not in the valid actions \u2014 only \u201cPeng T5\u201d and \u201cPass\u201d.\n\nSo you are not winning after Peng.\n\nTherefore, **Peng T5 leads to an incomplete hand with no pair**, just like now \u2014 but now you\u2019re forced to discard and stay waiting.\n\nBut perhaps you are waiting for J1 or T2?\n\nCurrent wait: you likely need to draw J1 or T2 to make a pair.\n\nWith Peng, you force yourself to discard one of them \u2014 so you go from holding both to discarding one \u2014 making it harder to complete the pair.\n\nFor example:\n- If you keep hand as is: you have T2, T5x2, J1 \u2192 if you draw J1 or T2, you can win with pair.\n- If you Peng T5 and discard, say, T2, then you\u2019re left with only J1 \u2014 so you need another J1 to pair.\n- Similarly, if you discard J1, you need another T2.\n\nBut unshown tiles:\n- T2: 4 copies total \u2014 none discarded visibly? Let