how is [T3,T4,T5]**, and you contributed T3 and T5 to it.\n\nThen you had one T5 used.\n\nYou discarded one T5.\n\nYou now have two T5s in hand.\n\nTotal: 1 (chow) + 1 (discard) + 2 (hand) = 4.\n\nSo the T5 being discarded now by previous player must be the fourth copy \u2014 but it's already in your hand.\n\nImpossible.\n\nTherefore, the only resolution is that **the T5 in the chow is not a physical T5 from your initial hand, but the discarded T5 was used in the chow**.\n\nNo: when you Chi, you use two from hand, one from discard.\n\nFor [T3,T4,T5], if you Chi T5, then you contribute T3 and T4.\n\nBut your history says \"Chi T4\", not \"Chi T5\".\n\n\"Chi T4\" means you are claiming a discarded T4 to complete a chow.\n\nSo the discarded tile is T4.\n\nYou contribute two from hand: either T2+T3, or T3+T5, or T5+T6.\n\nGiven you now have T2 and T5, likely you contributed T3 and T5 for [T3,T4,T5].\n\nSo you used one T3 and one T5 from hand.\n\nThen later, you drew tiles.\n\nYou discarded \"T5\" once.\n\nSo to have two T5s in hand now, you must have drawn three T5s after that \u2014 but only four exist.\n\nBreakdown:\n- Used in chow: 1 T5\n- Discarded by you: 1 T5\n- In hand now: 2 T5s\n= 4 T5s\n\nSo all T5s are accounted for.\n\nPrevious player cannot discard T5 \u2014 but they are, and Peng is valid.\n\nThis is a paradox.\n\nOnly explanation: the \"Play T5\" in your history is not a T5 suited tile, but something else? No, T5 is Bamboo 5.\n\nPerhaps the system allows it due to error, but we must proceed.\n\nAssume that Peng T5 is possible, meaning a T5 is available.\n\nPerhaps the chow [T3,T4,T5] used the discarded T5, not T4.\n\nBut your action was \"Chi T4\", which means you claimed a discarded T4.\n\nSo the tile discarded was T4.\n\nYou contributed T3 and T5.\n\nSo you used one T5.\n\nYou later discarded one T5.\n\nYou now have two T5s.\n\nThat's four.\n\nSo the current discard of T5 by previous player is the fourth copy \u2014 but it must be from somewhere.\n\nUnless your meld [T3,T4,T5] used a different combination.\n\nAnother possibility: you have a typo in meld.\n\nYour meld is written as [T3, T4, T5] \u2014 but maybe it's [T2, T3, T4], and you contributed T2 and T3, used discarded T4.\n\nThen you used one T2.\n\nYou now have one T2 in hand \u2014 so you must have had two initially.\n\nFine.\n\nYou used one T3.\n\nYou never had T5 in hand until later.\n\nYou drew two T5s.\n\nYou discarded one T5.\n\nSo you must have drawn three T5s.\n\nBut only four exist.\n\nDiscarded by you: 1\nIn hand: 2\nSo 3 accounted for.\n\nOne more somewhere.\n\nPrevious player discards T5 \u2014 that's the fourth.\n\nOK, possible.\n\nSo T5: \n- 1 discarded by you\n- 2 in your hand\n- 1 discarded by previous player (now)\n- and the chow [T2,T3,T4] does not use T5\n\nSo no T5 in chow.\n\nBut your meld is written as [T3, T4, T5] \u2014 explicitly.\n\nSo it does use T5.\n\nI think there is a data inconsistency, but we must proceed with given information.\n\nBest assumption: Peng T5 is offered, so it must be possible.\n\nPerhaps the chow is [T3,T4,T5], but the T5 in it is the one you are about to use \u2014 but no.\n\nFinal decision: despite the confusion, the only viable interpretation is that you can Peng T5, but doing so will not give you a pair, and you will have to discard either T2 or J1, both of which are potential pair candidates.\n\nSince you are not winning immediately, and you risk losing a chance to complete a pair, it is better to **pass** and keep your flexibility.\n\nMoreover, you have a rare opportunity to aim for higher-scoring hands:\n- You have three pungs already: F2, B7, and could have T5.\n- F2 is South Wind, prevalent wind is North (F4), your seat wind is East (F1).\n- So F2 is not prevalent or seat wind.\n- B7 is a simple pung.\n- But if you get another pair, you might score:\n  - All Pungs (6 points)\n  - Dragon Pung? You have no dragon pung.\n  - You have J1 in hand, could try to make a dragon pair.\n\nBut currently, best course is to preserve both T2 and J1.\n\nIf you Peng, you must discard one, reducing your chances.\n\nTherefore, **Pass is better**.\n\nEvaluation: Peng T5 allows forming a pung but forces discard of a potential pair tile (T2 or J1), while passing preserves flexibility to draw a pair. The hand is not winning after Peng, so there's no immediate gain.\n\nReason: Maintaining both T2 and J1 increases the probability of completing a pair on a future draw, whereas Peng T5 commits to discarding one of them, weakening the hand without immediate benefit.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
